1) 从告警标签取 pool，执行 curl -s http://127.0.0:
1) Take pool from the alert labels and run curl -s http://127.0.0.1/status?full to see each worker's state and the URI it is serving;
2) Many workers stuck on the same URI means that endpoint has slowed down; use the slowlog and application logs to locate it (database slow queries and external API timeouts are the usual causes);
3) In the short term raise pm.max_children to add concurrency, after confirming there is enough memory;
4) If the queue itself is too small (listen.backlog defaults to 511), raise it for your concurrency level and raise the kernel's net.core.somaxconn to match.

1) 从告警标签取 pool，执行 grep -i 'server reache:
1) Take pool from the alert labels and run grep -i 'server reached pm.max_children' /var/log/php-fpm.log to see how often this happens;
2) Compute the average per-process memory with ps --no-headers -o rss -C php-fpm | awk '{s+=$1} END {print s/NR/1024" MB"}', then derive a safe max_children from available memory divided by that figure;
3) Raise pm.max_children to the computed value and run systemctl reload php-fpm; do not raise it blindly or you will trigger an OOM;
4) If per-process memory or duration is abnormal, use the slowlog to find the slow scripts and reduce concurrency demand at the source.

1) 先确认是否是计划内发布或 reload；\n2) 非计划则执行 journ:
1) First confirm whether this was a planned release or reload;
2) If unplanned, run journalctl -u php-fpm --since '-30min' and tail -200 /var/log/php-fpm.log to find the cause;
3) Run dmesg -T | grep -i 'killed process' to check for an OOM kill, and if so lower pm.max_children based on real per-process memory;
4) For repeated restarts, check whether an extension is segfaulting by ruling out third-party extensions one by one with php -m.

1) 执行 curl -s http://127.0.0.1/status?fu:
1) Run curl -s http://127.0.0.1/status?full and watch the request duration of each worker to find processes held by long requests;
2) Decide whether concurrency has grown or a slow script is holding workers: scale out or raise pm.max_children for the former, fix the script for the latter;
3) With pm=dynamic, check whether pm.start_servers and pm.min_spare_servers are too low, which prevents new workers being spawned fast enough at peak;
4) For sustained high load, switch to pm=static with a process count computed from memory to avoid fork churn.

1) 执行 tail -100 /var/log/php-fpm/www-slo:
1) Run tail -100 /var/log/php-fpm/www-slow.log to read the full call stack of the slow requests; the top frame is where they are stuck;
2) Stacks stuck in PDO/mysqli point to the database, so check the slow query log and add indexes; stacks stuck in curl/file_get_contents need timeouts on the external call;
3) Stacks stuck in session_start are usually file-based session lock contention; switch to Redis sessions and call session_write_close early;
4) If request_slowlog_timeout is set too low and creates noise, adjust that parameter instead of ignoring the alert.
